# Measurement harness for {{SYSTEM_NAME}}

Generated by Scheduloscope {{VERSION}}. The C sources are fixed; only
`workload.h` and `workload_config.h` are derived from the model, so the
measurement logic cannot drift from what you reviewed.

## Build on QNX OS 8.0

```sh
source ~/qnx800/qnxsdp-env.sh
make qnx        # x86_64
make qnx-arm    # aarch64le, for a board
```

Both QNX targets compile with `-std=c11 -D_QNX_SOURCE`. Under strict ISO C,
`qcc` hides the POSIX and QNX OS declarations the harness uses unless
`_QNX_SOURCE` is defined.

`qnx-arm` builds only the AArch64 executable. `image.build.template` is an
x86_64 boot image template; there is no matching AArch64 image template, so a
board image needs its own startup and driver lines. The Scheduloscope repository
also records a validated AArch64 QEMU boot through a community MIT BSP; that is
runtime evidence, not an official QSTI image or a hardware-timing result:
https://github.com/fetachino/qnx-scheduloscope/blob/main/docs/verification.md.

Copy `scheduloscope-harness` to the target and run it with the privileges that
real-time priorities and priority inheritance require (root, or a process with
`PROCMGR_AID_PRIORITY`):

```sh
./scheduloscope-harness -d 30000 -o report.json
```

`-d` is the measurement window in milliseconds; the default is one hyperperiod
({{DURATION}}). `-o` writes JSON with the observed minimum, mean and maximum
response time per task, the activation latency of each job, and deadline-miss
and skipped-activation counts.

If a thread cannot be given its modelled policy and priority, the harness stops
with an explanatory error instead of returning numbers for a different system.

`image.build.template` builds a standalone x86_64 QNX BIOS IFS. It uses
`[image=8m]` and `[virtual=x86_64,bios] .bootstrap`, copies the harness, the
QNX 8 dynamic loader `ldqnx-64.so.2`, `libc.so.6`, `libgcc_s.so.1`, `libm.so.3`,
`libz.so.2`, `libtracelog.so.1`, `libsecpol.so.1` and `libfsnotify.so.1` into
`/proc/boot` (the default `mkifs` prefix), and creates the
`/usr/lib/ldqnx-64.so.2` link the ELF interpreter needs. It also installs real
copies under `/system/lib`: QNX 8 privileged services such as `devc-ser8250` and
`pipe` resolve their trusted library path there, not from `/proc/boot`, and
without it the serial manager reports `ldd:FATAL: Could not load library
libsecpol.so.1` and `/dev/ser1` never appears.

The template also includes the `devc-ser8250`, `pipe` and `toybox`/`cat`
commands named by the boot script; `devc-ser8250` runs with `&` because it is a
daemon and would otherwise block the script. The IFS is read-only, so `/tmp` is
a link to the kernel's writable `/dev/shmem`; the harness writes
`-o /tmp/report.json` there and the script prints it to `/dev/ser1`.

The template was built with QNX SDP 8.0.6, packaged as its own QNX6 boot
partition with `mkqnx6fsimg` and `diskimage`, and booted in QEMU. The guest
ran the 30 s window and wrote JSON with `"platform": "qnx"` and
`"scheduling": "enforced"`. The boot used `-accel tcg -cpu max`; KVM stalled
after the loader in that environment. See `docs/verification.md` for the
partition layout, exact command and captured output.

## Build and run on a POSIX host

```sh
make run
```

The host backend exists to prove that the harness compiles and runs. It is
**not** a real-time scheduler: Linux will not honour QNX priorities or QNX
sporadic replenishment. When the host refuses real-time scheduling the run
continues **best-effort** and the report says `"scheduling": "best-effort"`;
those numbers say nothing about on-target latency.

## What the harness reproduces

- one thread per task, with the modelled priority and policy (SCHED_FIFO,
  SCHED_RR, SCHED_SPORADIC with budget and replenishment);
- critical sections on mutexes created with the modelled protocol
  (PTHREAD_PRIO_INHERIT, PTHREAD_PRIO_PROTECT with ceiling, or none);
- RPCs as real channels (ChannelCreate/MsgSend/MsgReceive/MsgReply), with
  `_NTO_CHF_FIXED_PRIORITY` when the model asks for it;
- `sleep` segments as blocking sleeps;
- CPU work as calibrated busy-spin, so a segment consumes CPU instead of
  yielding the processor.

Response time is `completion - nominal release`, so it includes activation
latency, execution and blocking. `misses` counts completed jobs whose response
exceeded their deadline; `skipped` counts individual nominal periodic releases
that were never activated (one per miss, not one per starvation episode);
`deadline_misses` is their sum. A deadline of 0 means "none" and never misses;
omitting `deadline` produces 0, while an explicit zero is rejected by the parser.
All task threads arrive at a start gate before the first release, so creating a
thread late cannot shift another task's window. Servers stop on a distinct
stop pulse/message and are joined with a bound; the harness never runs a normal
handler for a stop. Activation latency and response time are
reported separately. An aperiodic task has no deadline by default: its
generated deadline is 0, so it reports response times but no deadline misses.
If the model gives an aperiodic task an explicit deadline, the harness measures
completions against it, but response-time analysis still makes no guarantee
about future arrival rates.

## What it does not reproduce

- **interrupt sources.** The harness runs threads, not ISR/IST handlers. Its
  response times exclude the interrupt interference the analysis includes, so
  compare them with the `ISR` column, not the `R` column. The JSON marks this
  as `"interrupts_reproduced": false` when the model has interrupt sources.
- `yield` and `setPriority` segments (Scheduloscope reports them when the model
  uses them);
- cache effects, memory pressure, DVFS, or the cost of the rest of your system;
- RPC timeouts (the harness blocks until the server replies);
- a guarantee for an aperiodic arrival pattern: an explicit deadline is an
  observed target, not a schedulability result.

Measure the CPU budget of each segment on the real board before trusting the
analysis. A harness number above the analytical bound means the model is wrong,
and the model is the thing to fix.
